.he "tail (1)                  9/30/26                        tail (1)"
.fo "                            -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
tail
- prints the last few lines of a file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
tail [ -N ] [ file ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Tail
prints the last n lines (default = 10) of the named file.
If no file is given, the last n lines before the first
end-of-file encountered on the standard input are printed.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The program is v7's tail, and the form above is what the 1982 page
fixes.  That much and no more: v7's argument grammar has more in it
than the page says, and the port kept it.
.sp
A leading
.bd
+
counts from the beginning of the file instead of the end, so
.bd
tail +3 file
prints everything from the third line on.  A letter after the number
says what is being counted:
.bd
l
for lines, which is the default,
.bd
b
for 512 byte blocks,
.bd
c
for characters, and
.bd
r
for the lines in reverse order from the end, as a filter that reads a
file backwards.
.sp
The 1982 binary is a different program and this is not it.  It keeps
its own messages - "line too long", "Out of memory" - and it reads the
whole file in, where this program seeks to the last 4096 bytes of it.
It also does not take -N at all: run it as "tail -3 file" and it exits
1 having written nothing and said nothing.  This port follows the page,
which documents -N, and does what the page says with it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.br
.in +5
Tail
holds the file in a buffer of 4096 bytes, so it can count back no
further than that.  Asking for more lines than the last 4096 bytes
hold prints only what is in the buffer, from the start of it, and the
lines before that are lost.  The 1982 page says the same of the
command it documents, in its own words: "If n is too large, tail will
fail since it stores the text of lines in memory."
.sp
A file whose last line has no newline is counted as one line longer
than it is, because the count runs on newlines and a last line without
one is not a line to this program.  "tail -2" on a three line file
that ends without a newline prints all three.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
cat (1), od (1), sed (1)
